  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 17,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.047,
            0.418,
            0.065
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8 | Part-II"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.087,
            0.627,
            0.131
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What is the sum of the first (n – 1) odd numbers? Thus,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.394,
            0.13,
            0.604,
            0.15
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_17_equation_2.jpg",
          "content": "(n - 1)^{2} + (2n - 1) = n^{2}."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.155,
            0.845,
            0.193
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note that this equation could also have been directly obtained by expanding "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( n - 1 ) ^ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and adding "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2 n - 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " to it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.192,
            0.846,
            0.249
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If the nth odd number, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2 n - 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , is also a square number, then we have a sum of two square numbers equal to another square number. We will use this idea to generate Baudhāyana triples."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.252,
            0.845,
            0.272
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. 9 is an odd square. It is the 5th odd number "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( 9 = 2 \\times 5 - 1 )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . So, we have"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.402,
            0.281,
            0.596,
            0.324
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_17_equation_6.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{array}{c}1+3+5+7+9=5^{2} \\\\ 4^{2}+3^{2}=5^{2}\\end{array}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.341,
            0.846,
            0.405
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Could we have obtained this triple using the equation "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "(n - 1)^{2} + (2n - 1) = n^{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Since we took the 5th odd number, the value of n is 5. Substituting "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n = 5"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " into the equation, we get"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.427,
            0.398,
            0.574,
            0.423
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_17_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "(5 - 1)^{2} + 9 = 5^{2}"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.444,
            0.431,
            0.554,
            0.451
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_17_equation_9.jpg",
          "content": "4^{2} + 3^{2} = 5^{2}."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.462,
            0.821,
            0.482
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. 25 is an odd square. It is the 13th odd number "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( 2 5 \\equiv 2 \\times 1 3 - 1 ) . \\; \\mathrm { S o } ,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.492,
            0.774,
            0.597
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.202,
                0.492,
                0.774,
                0.597
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_17_table_11.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tbody><tr><td>1 + 3 + 5 + … + 23 + 25 = 13<sup>2</sup>12<sup>2</sup> + 5<sup>2</sup> = 13<sup>2</sup></td><td>We have n = 13. Substituting this value in the equation, we get(13 - 1)<sup>2</sup> + 25 = 13<sup>2</sup>12<sup>2</sup> + 5<sup>2</sup> = 13<sup>2</sup></td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.607,
            0.294,
            0.63
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Figure it Out"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.64,
            0.655,
            0.66
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Find 5 more Baudhāyana triples using this idea."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.67,
            0.847,
            0.729
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Does this method yield non-primitive Baudhāyana triples? ["
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hint:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Observe that among the triples generated, one of the smaller sidelengths is one less than the hypotenuse.]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.738,
            0.802,
            0.778
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Are there primitive triples that cannot be obtained through this method? If yes, give examples."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.805,
            0.63,
            0.83
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.6 A Long-Standing Open Problem"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.838,
            0.847,
            0.897
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The study of Baudhāyana triples inspired the great French mathematician Fermat — who lived during the 17th century — to make a general statement about the sum of powers of positive integers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.816,
            0.73,
            0.903,
            0.785
          ],
